                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_perhour:       `<strong>That's the right lens.</strong> Contribution per constraint-hour, not per-unit margin: the module eats 1.9h per unit and the bracket only 0.6h. On a line where capacity binds, the per-hour number is the only one that tells you what to fill the constraint with.`,
                    coach_r1_perunit:       `<strong>Margin myopia.</strong> You're prioritizing the module at R$14/unit over the bracket at R$9. But the module eats 1.9h and the bracket 0.6h — that's R$7.4 vs R$15 per scarce hour. With 1.68M free hours, which one actually makes you more money?`,
                    coach_r1_revenue:       `<strong>Absolute revenue is the wrong yardstick here.</strong> The lines that invoice the most can be the worst per scarce hour. With capacity binding, only contribution-per-constraint-hour tells you where the value really is.`,
                    coach_r1_ebitda:        `<strong>Leaving the mix untouched is how you stay at 19% capture.</strong> Total EBITDA hides that 62% of your hours go to build-to-print work that captures R$6 of R$31. The whole point is to reallocate scarce hours, not freeze them.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> The largest create-vs-capture gap lives in build-to-print — R$6 captured of R$31 created — and you're ranking the scarce hours by per-hour contribution. That's exactly the lens that tells you what to stop and what to fill the constraint with.`,
                    coach_r1_gapmodule:     `<strong>The module isn't where the gap is.</strong> It already captures nearly double. The yawning gap is build-to-print: R$6 of R$31. That's the value you're leaving on the table because of position, not because of how you machine.`,
                    coach_r1_gapnone:       `<strong>That's how you stay commoditized.</strong> Capturing R$6 of R$31 you create is not "what you deserve" — it's what a build-to-print position lets you keep. The gap is real and it lives in build-to-print.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrank:       `<strong>Without ranking, you'll cut the wrong hours.</strong> If you don't rank the lines by contribution-per-constraint-hour first, you'll free capacity by sacrificing your best per-hour business. Do the ranking before you decide what to stop.`,
                    coach_r2_metrology:     `<strong>Metrology makes you the validated source.</strong> An advanced qualification cell is scarce and defensible — it's what justifies a 38% value share at the table. AGT can't just pay you the build-to-print rate when you're the one who can qualify the part.`,
                    coach_r2_coeng:         `<strong>A co-engineering cell is a real capability.</strong> It lets you co-develop rather than execute — a genuine claim on value. Slightly less defensible than being the sole validated source, but a long way from build-to-print.`,
                    coach_r2_assembly:      `<strong>Flexible assembly buys agility, not defensibility.</strong> Mix flexibility is easily replicated — the Paraná supplier can do it too. It won't anchor a larger capture share the way a scarce qualification capability would.`,
                    coach_r2_capnone:       `<strong>Capture without capability.</strong> You signed up for more value share but invested nothing that earns it. What exactly are you bringing that justifies it? Why wouldn't AGT just pay you the build-to-print rate?`,
                    coach_r2_planned:       `<strong>Capability plus the right displacement.</strong> You're building the scarce qualification capability AND freeing the worst per-hour commodity work to make room for the module. That's a coherent plan — capability backed by capacity.`,
                    coach_r2_exitagri:      `<strong>Wrong displacement.</strong> You dropped the agricultural line to make room — but it ran at R$13 per constraint-hour, higher than the module you kept. You freed capacity by sacrificing your best per-hour business. Did you rank what you cut?`,
                    coach_r2_exitnothing:   `<strong>Capacity binds — something has to give.</strong> Trying to fit the 2.4M-hour module ramp inside 1.68M free hours without stopping anything strands the ramp. You have to free the worst per-hour hours, not pretend it all fits.`,
                    coach_r2_capbust:       `<strong>You've already committed past R$40M.</strong> The board authorized R$40M, not R$57M, and your leverage sits near covenant. Phase it, lease the line — don't tell the board you ignored the cap in week one.`,
                    coach_r2_underfund:     `<strong>You're starving the capability you just chose.</strong> Reconfiguration + metrology + training cost R$22M. Underfunding it means the qualification cell never really lands — and an unbuilt capability earns no capture share.`,
                    coach_r3_band:          `<strong>That's a capability-backed claim.</strong> A 35–45% share is achievable because you built the scarce qualification capability that justifies it — and you're not over-reaching to the point AGT walks. This is capture earned, not demanded.`,
                    coach_r3_bandnobacking: `<strong>The number is fair, but what backs it?</strong> You're asking for a partner-band share without a scarce capability behind it. AGT can see there's nothing stopping them paying you the build-to-print rate. Capture share has to be earned first.`,
                    coach_r3_overreach:     `<strong>You held out for too big a slice.</strong> AGT just moved 40% of your volume — R$53.6 million — to the Paraná supplier and utilization dropped to 64%. You captured a bigger slice of a pie you no longer have. What's contribution-per-hour on idle capacity?`,
                    coach_r3_timid:         `<strong>You left value on the table.</strong> A scarce capability lets you claim the 35–45% partner band — asking for less than that just hands AGT the surplus you created. Anchor the share to the capability you built.`,
                    coach_r3_agtfunds:      `<strong>Letting AGT fund all the tooling weakens your claim.</strong> If you risk no relationship-specific capital, you look like a vendor, not a partner. Shared tooling is the credible signal that both sides are invested in the relationship.`,
                    coach_r3_impfunds:      `<strong>Funding all the tooling alone loads the risk on you.</strong> It signals commitment but strains the R$40M envelope and exposes you if volumes slip. Shared tooling splits the relationship-specific risk the way a partnership should.`,
                    coach_r3_exclusive:     `<strong>Exclusivity without firm volume is a trap.</strong> You've tied yourself to AGT with no guarantee they'll fill your constraint. Capture share on a pie AGT never commits to filling is capture share on idle capacity.`,
                    coach_r3_novolume:      `<strong>You didn't secure the volume.</strong> Capture share is worthless without the multi-year commitment that actually fills the bottleneck. Spot pricing leaves your scarce hours unfilled — and your fixed cost stranded.`,
                    coach_r4_phase:         `<strong>Disciplined sequencing.</strong> Reconfigure now for the capability that captures value, then relieve the ceiling via a covenant-safe lease. You stay inside the R$40M envelope and still ramp the module. That's investment discipline under a real constraint.`,
                    coach_r4_both:          `<strong>You've committed R$57M against a R$40M envelope.</strong> Your leverage already sits near covenant. Are you phasing this, leasing the line, or about to tell the board you ignored their cap in week one?`,
                    coach_r4_lineonly:      `<strong>You relieved capacity but built no capture.</strong> A new line without the qualification capability just lets you do more build-to-print. You've spent R$35M to stay commoditized at higher volume. Where's the capability that earns a larger share?`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>You took the safe path — toward commoditization.</strong> You've accepted a 4%/yr price-down on 28% of revenue and you're still capturing R$6 of R$31. In three years AGT re-bids and you have no differentiated capability to defend the work. Where does this end?`,
                    coach_r4_debt:          `<strong>Full new debt is the riskier path.</strong> Phasing was right, but loading new debt with leverage already near covenant is fragile. An operating lease preserves the covenant and aligns the cost to the module ramp.`,
                    coach_r4_defer:         `<strong>Deferring the relief risks the ramp.</strong> Phasing was right, but pushing capacity relief out a year leaves you bottlenecked exactly when the module ramps. A lease lets you relieve the ceiling now without busting the covenant.`,
                    coach_r4_exitagri:      `<strong>You exited your best per-hour business again.</strong> Agricultural ran at R$13 per constraint-hour. Freeing volume by cutting it weakens the business to make room. Cut by the per-hour ranking — keep the agri, drop the commodity brackets.`,
                    coach_r4_exitnone:      `<strong>The constraint never clears.</strong> If you exit nothing, the module ramp has nowhere to go inside your free hours. You have to free the worst per-hour volume — not hope it all fits.`,
                    coach_r4_weakpitch:     `<strong>A capture claim that isn't anchored won't survive the board.</strong> If you can't show the share rests on a real, scarce capability, the board hears wishful thinking. Anchor it to the qualification capability you built.`,

